WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.799 --> 00:00:05.590
Networks will put just about anything on television...from
a naked dating show to documenting a woman's

00:00:05.590 --> 00:00:06.590
600 pound life.

00:00:06.590 --> 00:00:10.800
The more extreme the better—and if you want
some serious bonus points, throw in some shocking

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:11.800
storylines.

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:14.530
Here are a few of TV's most controversial
plots.

00:00:14.530 --> 00:00:16.779
Game Of Thrones’ sexual assault

00:00:16.779 --> 00:00:20.350
No stranger to controversy, one of Game of
Thrones’ season five episodes left fans

00:00:20.350 --> 00:00:21.430
calling for a boycott.

00:00:21.430 --> 00:00:25.789
Sansa Stark, played by Sophie Turner, was
sexually assaulted on her wedding night by

00:00:25.789 --> 00:00:30.220
her new husband Ramsay Bolton—who also forces
Sansa's childhood friend, Theon Greyjoy, to

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:31.229
watch from the corner.

00:00:31.229 --> 00:00:32.229
Tough to watch?

00:00:32.229 --> 00:00:33.229
Yes.

00:00:33.229 --> 00:00:34.229
Tough to act out?

00:00:34.229 --> 00:00:35.229
Apparently not.

00:00:35.229 --> 00:00:36.229
Turner revealed to Entertainment Weekly,

00:00:36.229 --> 00:00:38.050
"When I read that scene, I kinda loved it.

00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:39.719
I love the way Ramsay had Theon watching.

00:00:39.719 --> 00:00:41.739
It was all so messed up.

00:00:41.739 --> 00:00:42.739
"

00:00:42.739 --> 00:00:45.949
Fans did not all agree, and US Senator Claire
McCaskill decided to publicly announce a boycott

00:00:45.949 --> 00:00:46.949
of the show.

00:00:46.949 --> 00:00:49.230
The Newsroom's campus rape

00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:53.820
In HBO's The Newsroom, Don, played by Thomas
Sadoski, goes to a college campus to interview

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:58.160
a female student who had created a website
to out rapists anonymously after she was raped

00:00:58.160 --> 00:00:59.160
herself.

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:02.300
Don is supposed to find out more information
about the woman, but, for many reasons, he

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:05.760
ends up discouraging her from going on his
news show to debate her attacker.

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:08.520
This led to an attack on series creator, Aaron
Sorkin.

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:12.160
According to The New York Times, viewers felt
Sorkin was victim-blaming women and showing

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:15.800
more concern about the after-effects of a
man who was wrongly accused of rape.

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:17.950
Sorkin responded in an interview with Observer,
saying,

00:01:17.950 --> 00:01:22.180
“I knew that one of the stories was controversial
and I’m not someone who courts controversy

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:25.560
so I was a little surprised by the vitriol
and misunderstanding this morning of what

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:30.050
was going on in that episode and terrible
inferences drawn from it about me personally.”

00:01:30.050 --> 00:01:31.450
Sons Of Anarchy’s school shooting

00:01:31.450 --> 00:01:35.630
There are a lot of over-the-top, gruesome
scenes in Sons of Anarchy, but when a young

00:01:35.630 --> 00:01:39.830
boy brings a KG-9 machine gun to his elementary
school, opening fire on his classmates and

00:01:39.830 --> 00:01:43.180
teachers—that's when fans thought the show
really crossed a line.

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:46.770
The episode aired just nine months after the
Sandy Hook Elementary School shooting, so

00:01:46.770 --> 00:01:49.580
the scene hit a little too close to home for
some.

00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:52.680
Series creator Kurt Sutter defended the value
of the scene to TV Guide, saying,

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:56.270
“I believe that it's about mental illness,
which is why I layered that in.

00:01:56.270 --> 00:01:59.430
I believe it's about parent neglect, which
is why I layered that in.

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:02.100
I believe it's about the gun laws and illegal
guns on the street.

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:04.740
All those things contribute to that perfect
storm.”

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:07.260
The Ellen Show’s coming out episode

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:11.680
In April 1997, Ellen DeGeneres and her sitcom,
The Ellen Show, changed television history

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:16.610
when her character, also named Ellen and based
on herself, came out on national television.

00:02:16.610 --> 00:02:20.310
Even though she’d only publicly come out
a few days earlier in Time Magazine, it was

00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:22.780
still a risky move for the late '90s.

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:26.350
Only a handful of scripted shows starred gay
characters, and the approval rating for gay

00:02:26.350 --> 00:02:28.880
marriage was at a low 27 percent.

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:32.450
Advertisers reportedly started pulling their
ads from the show, although only Chrysler

00:02:32.450 --> 00:02:36.390
actually admitted to it, citing a corporate
policy to avoid controversial clients.

00:02:36.390 --> 00:02:40.100
While coming out on TV wasn't the popular
thing to do at the time, Ellen said she didn't

00:02:40.100 --> 00:02:43.170
do it to make herself a standard-bearer for
the gay community—she just had to do it

00:02:43.170 --> 00:02:44.530
for herself.

00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:46.230
South Park and Muhammad

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:48.600
South Park is not afraid to go to extremes
to get laughs.

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:52.620
But when the show's creators, Matt Stone and
Trey Parker, tried using an image of the Islamic

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:55.290
prophet Muhammad, it was like all hell broke
loose.

00:02:55.290 --> 00:03:00.430
"Well after that episode ran, the following
quote appeared on a pro-jihadi website targeting

00:03:00.430 --> 00:03:02.060
the creators of South Park.

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:07.490
'We have to warn Matt and Trey,' they are
the creators, 'that what they are doing is

00:03:07.490 --> 00:03:10.790
stupid and they will probably wind up like
Theo Van Gogh.'"

00:03:10.790 --> 00:03:14.380
Though an image of Muhammad had aired before,
the network blocked the showrunners from using

00:03:14.380 --> 00:03:17.080
a visual of the prophet due to recent world
events and safety concerns.

00:03:17.080 --> 00:03:21.500
This did not make Stone or Parker happy campers,
so rather than showing a picture of Muhammad,

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:25.700
they posted a message onscreen reading, "Comedy
Central has refused to broadcast an image

00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:27.290
of Muhammad on their network."

00:03:27.290 --> 00:03:28.340
Thanks for watching!

00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:30.940
